molly: Quick ping — the README in `examples/web-quickstart` still shows the old auth pattern (copy/pasted by a few customers). It uses `Authorization: Bearer <API_KEY>` and `require('redwood').Redwood(...)`, which is out of date. Causing confusion during onboarding.

raj: Yep saw a support ticket this morning about the same exact paste. Error was `TypeError: Redwood is not a constructor` for node samples.

sam: Repro steps I ran: clone, `npm install`, paste env var as `REDWOOD_API_KEY=xyz`, start. SDK now expects `const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY })` so both the constructor name and env var changed.

jess: Oof — that's onboarding friction. New hires also hit it in the internal demo. We should update README + the template used by `create-redwood-app` so future projects don't copy the bad snippet.

molly: I'm drafting a tiny PR. Plan: replace old header snippet with the new constructor example, update env var name in README and note the breaking change in a short callout. Also add a note about token expiry: `client.on('auth:expired', ...)` -> actually SDK uses `client.auth.refresh()` now.

raj: Suggestion: add a minimal retry example too — people paste naive code and fail on transient 401s during CI token rotations. Something like:
```js
// recommended pattern
const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY });
async function generate() {
  for (let i=0;i<2;i++) {
    try {
      return await client.text.generate({ prompt: 'hi' });
    } catch (err) {
      if (err.code === 'AUTH_EXPIRED' && i === 0) {
        await client.auth.refresh();
        continue;
      }
      throw err;
    }
  }
}
```

sam: I'll add an integration test that runs the example with a fake expiring token to prevent regressions. I can wire it into the docs CI.

jess: Could we also add a short migration note in the changelog so customers searching for `REDWOOD_API_KEY` find it quickly?

molly: Good call. I'll add a changelog entry and a one-line migration note in the README. Also adding a tiny blurb reminding folks not to copy secret literals into public repos (:eyes:).

docbot: CI: docs-examples/test-examples failed on branch patch/readme-auth-fix — failing test `examples/web-quickstart:node-start` (exit code 1). Full logs: https://ci.internal.redwood/tests/1234

raj: I'll take the CI failure — likely because one of the examples used `module.exports = Redwood` in an older file. I'll sweep the codebase for `REDWOOD_API_KEY` and old constructor names.

sam: PR linked: https://github.com/redwood-inference/examples/pull/89 — replaces header snippet, updates env var references, adds retry snippet and test.

jess: Thanks team. Quick win for DX. Can we also add a doc linter rule to flag `Authorization: Bearer` header blocks that include `REDWOOD_API_KEY` string? Just to catch future regressions.

molly: Yep adding a small docs-mdcheck rule now. Will merge after CI green. :thumbsup:
